import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The server",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform: Roc compiled to native code in a Rust host, as one executable. How to run it, its flags and limits, its log; how each request runs as a call that pauses while it waits, how a crash ends one call only, how pages are rendered, and how plugins add to an app.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "running", label: "Running it" },
		{ id: "logging", label: "The log" },
		{ id: "deploying", label: "Deploying" },
		{ id: "layers", label: "What is Rust, what is Roc" },
		{ id: "calls", label: "A call pauses while it waits" },
		{ id: "crashes", label: "A crash ends one call" },
		{ id: "memory", label: "Memory" },
		{ id: "streams", label: "Streams of events" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "Pages, rendered on the server" },
		{ id: "background", label: "Background loops and isolated calls" },
		{ id: "plugins", label: "Plugins" },
		{ id: "tcp", label: "Sockets and randomness" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the server"
				title="Roc compiled to native code, in a Rust host"
			>
				An app's server is one executable: your Roc, compiled to machine code, linked with a host written
				in Rust (on tokio and hyper). The host answers HTTP, runs your code for each request, catches a
				crash so it ends only that request, and frees the memory it used. Your Roc decides what every
				request gets; the host carries it out. This page covers running the server, then how it works
				inside.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="running">Running it</Heading>
			<p>
				While you develop, <code>rocx dev</code> builds the server and runs it for you, with
				<code>--dev</code>. A built executable takes these options; the environment variable in
				parentheses, where there is one, does the same as the flag:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="The app's executable"
				source={"<app> --port 8080 --host 127.0.0.1        where to listen (PORT)\n      --static DIR, --file /PATH=FILE     files served as they are\n      --context-file FILE                 what the app's init! is given, once, as it starts\n      --env-file FILE                     settings for the environment, read as it starts (none without it: a .env here isn't read unless named); the environment wins\n      --database-url URL                  the database (DATABASE_URL): postgres://…, or sqlite:<path> (turso:<path>) for a SQLite (Turso) app's file elsewhere\n      --actor-idle-ms 900000              an actor idle this long is let go (0: never; kept in the database)\n      --kv-max-mb 64                      Kv's limit (KV_MAX_MB): the least recently used goes past it\n      --max-body 16MiB                    the most a request's body may be (MAX_BODY): bytes, or KiB, MiB, GiB; 413 beyond\n      --public-url https://app.example    where the app is, for links it sends (PUBLIC_URL; else where it listens)\n      --dev                               reload pages on restart, show crashes, plugins' dev pages (/__rocx/mail)\n      --debug                             GET /__rocx/allocations and /__rocx/plugin-files, and plugins' debug paths\n      --live-beat-ms 15000                how often a channel stream beats, and its checks run again\n      --roc-threads N                     the threads Roc runs on (ROCX_ROC_THREADS; one a core)"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Request bodies.</strong> The host reads a request's body whole before your code runs, so
					there is always a limit: 16 MiB by default, or what <code>--max-body</code> says (a number of
					bytes, or of KiB, MiB or GiB, such as <code>64MiB</code>). A larger body is answered 413.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Static files.</strong> <code>--static</code> and <code>--file</code> serve files as they
					are, typed by their extension (with <code>cache-control: no-store</code> under
					<code>--dev</code>). A file directly under <code>/rocx/</code> whose name ends in a dash and 16
					hex digits (<code>styles-0123456789abcdef.css</code>, the stylesheet rocx build writes) is named
					by its content, so the browser may keep it for a year
					(<code>cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Proxies.</strong> Requests the app makes to other servers go through
					<code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> or <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> when set, except to hosts
					<code>NO_PROXY</code> names (<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#connections"}>HTTP</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Starting.</strong> The server first reads <code>.env</code>, if there is one, into its
					environment (<a href={Paths.platform_settings}>settings</a>). Then it takes its port: if the port
					is in use, it stops there, before it opens the database or runs anything. Then it opens the
					database, checks the plugins, applies the migrations, runs the app's <code>init!</code>, starts
					the background loops, and answers requests.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Stopping.</strong> On SIGTERM or Ctrl-C, the server stops listening and taking new jobs,
					then waits up to ten seconds for the requests, streams and jobs still running.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Without a database</strong> (a Postgres app with no URL), a server whose app or plugins
					have tables (migrations) doesn't start, and says which have them, rather than start and fail at
					every query. Under <code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code> it starts, and a line on the log
					says so. An app with no tables starts either way; what needs the database says so when it is
					used.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="logging">The log</Heading>
			<p>
				Every line the server writes to stderr is an event: a level, a message, and fields. Your app's
				lines, the host's (starting, stopping, a crash with its method, URL and error) and the plugins'
				(a migration applied, a job that failed) all go through one formatter. Messages stay fixed and
				the details go in fields, so a log collector can filter on them. Log with <code>Log.info!</code>,
				<code>Log.debug!</code>, <code>Log.warn!</code> or <code>Log.error!</code>, giving a message and a
				record of fields (<code>{"{}"}</code> for none); <code>Log.line!</code> logs a message alone. The
				format is set in the program:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An event, and the log's format"
				source={"Log.info!(\"gate failed\", { path: request.path, error: why })\n\nprogram = {\n\t..App.program({ init!, middleware }),\n\tlogs: { format: Auto }, # or Pretty, or Json\n}"}
			/>
			<Code
				title="The line, pretty and as JSON"
				source={"14:03:12 INFO  gate failed error=\"no session\" path=/admin\n{\"time\":\"2026-11-21T13:03:12.345Z\",\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"gate failed\",\"error\":\"no session\",\"path\":\"/admin\"}"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Auto</code>, the default, uses <code>ROCX_LOG</code> (<code>pretty</code> or
				<code>json</code>) if it is set. Otherwise it writes pretty lines, with the level in colour, when
				stderr is a terminal, and JSON when it isn't (a server whose log is collected). A field named
				<code>time</code>, <code>level</code> or <code>msg</code> is written as <code>fields.msg</code> and
				so on, so it can't replace the line's own.
			</p>
			<p>
				Logging never waits for stderr. Each event goes on a queue, and a thread of its own writes the
				lines in order, flushing when the queue is empty and before the process exits. If the queue is
				full (64k events), the caller waits rather than lose a line.
			</p>

			<Heading id="deploying">Deploying</Heading>
			<p>
				A release build is one command: <code>rocx build</code> writes
				<code>dist/server</code> and <code>dist/static</code> (<a href={"${Paths.tooling}#commands"}>rocx</a>).
				<code>--target arm64musl</code> (or <code>x64musl</code>) makes a static Linux executable, from
				macOS too: the platform's prebuilt host carries musl's C library and unwinder. The migrations are
				inside the executable. To deploy:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Put it in a container with the app's static files: <code>rocx build --image</code> writes an image with nothing else in it, no Dockerfile needed, for <code>docker load -i my-app.tar</code> (<a href={"${Paths.tooling}#commands"}>rocx</a>). Its server has the database engine the app's program names alone, and no debug info: a hello world's image is 3.7 MB to send.</li>
				<li>Give it its database: a Postgres app a <code>DATABASE_URL</code>; a SQLite app a file on a volume (<code>--database-url sqlite:/data/app.db</code>).</li>
				<li>Give it its settings (<code>APP_SECRET</code>, <code>SMTP_URL</code>, an API's key) as environment variables, or in a file it reads as it starts (<code>--env-file</code>; the environment wins). A plugin whose setting is missing stops the start, saying which.</li>
				<li>Put a proxy in front of it for TLS, and tell the app its public address with <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>, so the links it sends point there (<a href={Paths.platform_settings}>settings</a>).</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The platform's test app, built this way, runs all of its tests in a container against a Postgres.
			</p>
			<p>
				An app whose pages need no server once they are rendered (docs, a blog, a landing page) can be
				deployed as files instead: <code>rocx build app --static dist</code> writes every page, its data
				and what it loads, for GitHub Pages or a CDN (<a href={"${Paths.tooling}#static"}>a static
				copy</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="layers">What is Rust, what is Roc</Heading>
			<p>
				The host gives Roc general building blocks. Everything an app is built from on top of them is Roc,
				in the platform or in packages, and an app could write its own the same way:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Rust</strong> (<code>server/host</code>): HTTP; threads; the database (a pool of
					Postgres connections plus one that listens for notifications, or SQLite's file in the
					process, its calls on threads of their own); time zones; TCP and TLS sockets; live topics and
					rooms; channels' streams; background loops and isolated calls; actors' mailboxes and timers;
					values in memory (<code>Kv</code>); secure random numbers, AES-GCM and P-256; reading
					<code>.env</code>; and rendering pages of components.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Roc</strong> (<code>server/platform/*.roc</code>): migrations, plugins (checking and
					running them), actors' state and messages, and decoding database rows. Jobs and cron, and email
					(an SMTP client, MIME), are Roc too, built on the same effects, in packages
					(<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>packages/jobs</a>, <a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>packages/email</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Every effect each module offers, with its type, is in the <a href={Paths.platform_reference}>platform
				reference</a>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="calls">A call pauses while it waits</Heading>
			<p>
				Your Roc runs on threads of its own: one per CPU core, unless <code>--roc-threads</code> (or
				<code>ROCX_ROC_THREADS</code>) says otherwise. They sit beside tokio's threads, which handle HTTP
				and every socket and timer. Each call into Roc (a request and the page it renders, a stream, an
				actor's turn, a background loop) runs as a <strong>coroutine</strong> on one of Roc's threads,
				with a stack of its own.
			</p>
			<p>
				When an effect has to wait (a query, a socket, another server, a sleep), the call pauses. Its stack
				stays as it is, its thread runs other calls meanwhile, and the call resumes where it left off, on
				the same thread, once what it waited for is done. So a waiting call holds no thread, and any number
				of calls can wait at once. The tests check this: eight requests that each wait 0.3 s on Postgres
				take about 0.3 s together, and 2,000 requests that each wait a second, on two threads, take about a
				second.
			</p>
			<p>This has costs:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Roc that computes for a long time without an effect (a long pure loop) can't pause, so the calls
					queued on its thread wait for it. New calls go to Roc's other threads.
				</li>
				<li>
					A call with a time limit (a job's) is still ended when its time is up: the server interrupts its
					thread and, when the thread is in the app's code, ends the call as a crash would.
				</li>
				<li>
					Each stack reserves 8 MiB (Roc's default, since a page is a deep tree to render), but a paused
					call costs only the memory pages its stack actually touched.
				</li>
				<li>Profilers and debuggers see calls on stacks of their own rather than on threads.</li>
				<li>A call that overflows its stack still ends the process, saying so on stderr first.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Each call has its own context: its open transaction, the rooms it joined, the topics it listens
				to, and its sockets. All of it is given back when the call ends. A value Roc makes belongs to its
				call until the call returns; after that the host owns it and may move it to another call (a
				stream, to the call that sends it), but never shares it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="crashes">A crash ends one call</Heading>
			<p>
				In Roc, a <code>crash</code> or an integer overflow stops the program. Roc's ABI lets the host
				decide what that means: <code>roc_crashed</code> must never return, so it may either end the
				process or jump out of the Roc code that called it. This host does the second. A guard
				(<code>server/host/src/guard.c</code>) sets a jump target around every call into Roc; a crash jumps
				back to it, and only that call ends:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>A request</strong> is answered 500 (with the crash's message under <code>--dev</code>).</li>
				<li><strong>A stream</strong> ends.</li>
				<li><strong>A job</strong> counts as failed and runs again later.</li>
				<li><strong>An actor's turn</strong> is logged and reported to whoever asked; the actor's state stays as it was.</li>
				<li><strong>A transaction</strong> the call had open is rolled back.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The frames a crash skips are only Roc's and a small trampoline's, never the host's, so no Rust
				destructor is skipped.
			</p>

			<Heading id="memory">Memory</Heading>
			<p>
				A crash must not leak memory. While a call runs, every allocation it makes is linked into a list
				that belongs to the call. If the call returns, the answer's strings are copied out and the list
				is freed (a stream keeps its list until the call that sends it is done). If the call crashes,
				everything in its list is freed. Roc's reference counts are atomic in native builds, so a value
				moved to another thread stays correct.
			</p>
			<p>
				The tests crash 200 requests at once, half of them after allocating 2,000 strings, and check
				afterwards that Roc holds no allocation (<code>--debug</code> serves the count at
				<code>/__rocx/allocations</code>). This site's tests render its pages again and again and check the
				same.
			</p>

			<Heading id="streams">Streams of events</Heading>
			<p>
				An endpoint can answer with a stream of server-sent events: a loop,
				<code>{"Events.stream(|{ send!, sleep! }| …)"}</code>. The host runs it as a call of its own for as
				long as it is open. <code>send!</code> writes an event to the response, and <code>sleep!</code>
				pauses. When the client goes away, <code>sleep!</code> wakes early and the next
				<code>send!</code> returns <code>Err(Gone)</code>, so <code>?</code> ends the loop. A sleeping
				stream holds no thread: the tests keep 2,000 open on two threads, and a request beside them is
				still answered at once. <a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>Events</a> shows one, with a component
				listening to it.
			</p>
			<p>
				For live data that pages follow, a <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#channels"}>channel</a> is
				usually better: all of a tab's channels share one stream, the host keeps that stream without a
				call, and Roc runs only briefly, for a channel's check or hook.
			</p>

			<Heading id="pages">Pages, rendered on the server</Heading>
			<p>
				A page is one tree of components: its layouts, the page, and what they contain. The host renders
				it whole (<code>server/host/src/signals.rs</code>) exactly as the browser's runtime would: each
				component runs once, each hole runs once, nothing is tracked and nothing waits, and a memo is
				computed when it is made. The output carries small HTML comments that mark where each hole is, so
				the browser can hydrate it. Markup inside <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> is rendered as plain HTML,
				with no markers: the browser never hydrates it. The site's tests compare what the host renders with
				what the browser's runtime renders, byte for byte, on every page.
			</p>
			<p>
				When a component reads an endpoint (<code>Endpoint.read!</code>, a GET of one of the app's own
				paths), the host makes that request in-process: it runs through the app's <code>respond!</code>,
				behind the middleware of the route's directories, with <code>accept: application/json</code>, the page's headers
				(cookies included) and the app's context. So the component gets the same answer the browser would.
				Each URL is asked once per render. The answers go into a script at the end of the page, each under
				its URL, followed by <code>/rocx/start.js</code>, and the browser reads them from there instead of
				asking again. Reads made inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> aren't recorded, since the browser
				never makes them.
			</p>
			<p>
				When something fails (a context nothing provides, a memo that failed, an endpoint that answers
				anything but 200), the nearest <code>{"<Errored>"}</code> shows its fallback, as in the browser. A
				redirect from an endpoint makes the page that redirect. A crash ends the render, and the page is
				answered 500.
			</p>

			<Heading id="background">Background loops and isolated calls</Heading>
			<p>
				As it starts, the server asks the platform's Roc which loops to run in the background: the
				plugins' loops (the jobs plugin's workers, its schedules and its hourly sweep of finished jobs) and
				their checks (the email plugin's look at the SMTP server). Each runs as a call of its own until
				the server stops; one that crashes starts again a second later. A loop sleeps with
				<code>Live.next!</code>, which wakes when something is published on a topic it listens to, when its
				time is up, or with <code>Gone</code> when the server stops. <code>Db.notify!</code> (and Postgres's
				<code>NOTIFY</code>) publish on <code>{"db:<channel>"}</code>, which is how job workers sleep until
				a job is queued.
			</p>
			<p>
				An <strong>isolated call</strong> runs Roc inside another call, guarded on its own: a crash in it
				ends it alone, and the caller is told why. Plugins run calls this way
				(<code>Plugin.isolated!</code>); the jobs plugin runs each job so, which is why a job that crashes
				is just a job that failed, and the worker goes on.
			</p>
			<p>
				An actor's turn is a guarded call of its own, too. Its mailbox, in the host, runs it with the kind,
				key, state and message as strings, so a crash frees all of them. No lock is taken: one task routes
				every message to its actor's mailbox, and each actor handles its own messages one at a time
				(<a href={Paths.platform_actors}>actors</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="plugins">Plugins</Heading>
			<p>
				A <strong>plugin</strong> is a package that adds to an app what only the server runs: routes,
				tables, background loops, files, scripts every page loads, development pages, work at start,
				checks. You install one in a line: name the package in the app's header, and list its plugin in
				the program. The jobs, email, auth, push, Stripe and audio packages, among others, are
				plugins.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\", acme: \"../packages/acme/main.roc\" }\n\nimport acme.Acme\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!,\n\tplugins: [Acme.plugin({ at: \"/acme\" })],\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				A plugin is one value, a <code>Plugin.Value</code>, which its package makes from options of its
				own. What it adds:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Routes</strong> answer inside the app's middleware, given what the middleware hands on
					(the signed-in user, say). A plugin's route can't share a method and path with the app's.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tables</strong> (its migrations) are applied after the platform's and before the app's,
					each recorded under the plugin's name. A plugin may list one migration per database flavor.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Calls, loops, starts and checks</strong> are given the app's context, which its
					<code>init!</code> made. The plugin doesn't need to know its type
					(<code>Plugin.Value(context, ctx)</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Needs</strong> say what the compiler can't check: an environment variable, a database
					(any, or Postgres or SQLite only), another plugin.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Files and scripts</strong>, below.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				As it starts, the server checks every plugin at once, and any problem stops it, with the whole
				list: two routes at one path (the app's and a plugin's, or two plugins'), a file at the root where a
				route is, two files at one place, a name used twice, an environment variable a plugin needs and
				doesn't have, a database it needs, a plugin it needs that isn't listed. Then it logs what each
				plugin added.
			</p>
			<p>
				A plugin package names the server platform in its header, as an app does. The modules the browser
				can compile too are listed in a second header, <code>browser.roc</code>, which the browser's copy
				of the app uses instead.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>A plugin's files</strong> are embedded when the app is built
				(<code>{"import \"static/push-sw.js\" as worker : List(U8)"}</code>) and handed to the host once,
				at start. The host serves them before anything else sees the request, the app's middleware
				included, so an app's sign-in guard never blocks a service worker or a script. A file is served at
				<code>{"/rocx/plugins/<plugin>/<path>"}</code>, or at <code>{"/<path>"}</code> with
				<code>at: Root</code> (a service worker, whose scope is where it is). It answers <code>GET</code>
				and <code>HEAD</code>, with its content type and an ETag of its bytes (304 when the browser has
				them already). Files listed as <code>scripts</code> are loaded by every page the server renders,
				each as a module before <code>start.js</code>. Such a script can register browser effects that
				<code>Browser.call!</code> finds by the name <code>{"<plugin>.<effect>"}</code>; each is given
				<code>{"{ under }"}</code>, which puts a URL under the site's base path. The
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>push</a> plugin serves its service worker at the root and its script
				this way, and the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>audio</a> plugin its player.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="A plugin's files"
				source={"import \"static/push-sw.js\" as worker : List(U8)\nimport \"static/push.js\" as script : List(U8)\n\nfiles: [\n\t{ path: \"push-sw.js\", at: Root, content_type: \"text/javascript\", body: worker },\n\t{ path: \"push.js\", at: Plugin, content_type: \"text/javascript\", body: script },\n],\nscripts: [\"push.js\"],"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>

			<Heading id="tcp">Sockets and randomness</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Tcp.connect!</code> (or <code>connect_tls!</code>, or <code>start_tls!</code> on an open
				socket) opens a socket for the call. <code>write_bytes!</code>, <code>read_exactly!</code>,
				<code>read_up_to!</code> and <code>read_until!</code> use it, the call pausing while each waits, and
				<code>close!</code> closes it; a socket the call leaves open is closed when the call ends. TLS is
				rustls, checked against the web's certificate authorities. <code>Random.bytes!</code> and
				<code>Random.seed_u64!</code> are the operating system's secure randomness
				(<a href={Paths.platform_crypto}>crypto</a>). The email package's SMTP client is written on these,
				and so is <a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#roc-pg"}>roc-pg</a>, a Postgres client in Roc.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>HTTP/1.1 only, and no TLS:</strong> put the server behind a proxy that terminates TLS.</li>
				<li><strong>A stack overflow in Roc</strong> still ends the process (it says so first).</li>
				<li><strong>One instance:</strong> live state, <code>Kv</code> and actors' mailboxes are in one process's memory, so an app isn't meant to run as several copies.</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Built on">
				<p>
					tokio and hyper (HTTP), corosensei (coroutines), tokio-postgres with deadpool, rusqlite with
					SQLite compiled in, turso_core (Turso's engine; a release has Postgres's and Turso's only if
					its program names them), chrono-tz (time zones), rustls with ring (TLS;
					ring for random numbers, AES-GCM and P-256 too), and Roc's glue for the types the platform's
					header names (<code>just glue</code> writes <code>server/host/src/roc_platform_abi.rs</code>).
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
